  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Localized heavy rainfall/gusty outflow wind threat today with \n   showers and storms.\n\n- Warmest temperatures so far this summer possible this weekend \n  into early next week\n\nThe most impactful weather of the week arrives today as a robust \nsurge of subtropical moisture moves into the region. Combined \nwith afternoon heating, this moisture will spark organized \nthunderstorms across north-central Idaho and west-central into \nsouthwest Montana. Because these storms are anticipated to be \nslow-moving, rainfall could stack up significantly in a short \nperiod of time. Primary hazards include frequent lightning capable\nof igniting new wildfires in dry fuels, as well as erratic wind \ngusts of 40 to 50 mph. Notably, high-resolution models show these \nwinds tracking north through the Bitterroot and Missoula valleys \nbetween 5:00 PM and 7:00 PM with gusts up to 40 mph possible. \nAdditionally, heavy rainfall rates of 0.50 to 0.75 inches per hour\nwill introduce a localized risk for flash flooding and debris \nflows, particularly over recent burn scars.\n\nWhile thick morning cloud cover may initially delay daytime \nstorm development, strong atmospheric forcing is expected to \novercome this by the afternoon. Showers and isolated thunderstorms\nmay linger overnight, particularly across northwest Montana and \nalong the Continental Divide. By Tuesday, lower elevations will \nbegin to dry out, but lingering mid-level moisture will trigger a \nsecond round of scattered afternoon thunderstorms. This activity \nwill be focused primarily from Interstate 90 southward and along \nthe Continental Divide.\n\nWednesday through Friday, the region transitions back to a classic \nsummer pattern. Expect warm, dry conditions with breezy westerly \nafternoon winds. Any isolated, terrain-driven afternoon \nthunderstorms will be largely restricted to the mountains of \nsouthwest Montana.\n\nLooking ahead to next weekend and into mid-July, forecast models \nindicate that a strong ridge of high pressure currently over the \nDesert Southwest will expand northward. While the ridge's exact \nplacement remains uncertain, the prevailing trend strongly favors \na shift toward well above normal temperatures. As this prolonged \nheat builds late in the week and through the weekend, the risk for\nheat-related illnesses will increase. This will be especially \ntrue for vulnerable populations and those recreating outdoors \nwithout adequate hydration and shade. Forecast models also suggest\nthis high-pressure setup could periodically draw moisture up from\nthe southwest beginning around July 13th or 14th, potentially \ntriggering a return to daily thunderstorm activity.",
